  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWarm temperatures continue today into Wednesday ahead of a cold \nfront. Light rain is possible along the front late Wednesday into \nThursday morning, mainly north of Highway 2 in northeast North \nDakota and far northwest Minnesota. Cooler temperatures are expected \nThursday, with a slight rebound in temps on Friday, mainly in west \ncentral Minnesota. Another cold front and upper low will traverse \nthe Northern Plains and Upper Midwest Friday into Saturday, bringing \nadditional chances for light rain and light snow. Much colder air \nsurges southward late Saturday into Sunday, with highs on Sunday in \nthe 20s to low 30s. These cooler temperatures extend into at least \nthe first half of next week.\n\n...Rain and Snow Friday into Saturday...\n\nThe latest guidance is showing a slightly weaker, more progressive \nupper low, with some members keeping the low from closing \naltogether. At this time, there is a better chance for rain in the \nwarm sector of the system, which will cover most locations south of \nHighway 200. Further north, there remains some uncertainty regarding \np-type, with northeast North Dakota seeing the best chances for \naccumulating snow. The probability for 2 inches of snow or greater \nfor northeast North Dakota is currently around 20-30 percent, with \nchances quickly diminishing further south and east. Most areas will \nsee at least a brief transition to snow; however, due to relatively \nwarm surface/ground temps, melting is likely to overcome \naccumulation in all but the heaviest snow bands. Winds are slightly \nlower in the latest guidance in response to the expectation of a \nweaker upper low. Northwest wind speeds on Saturday could range from \n15-25 mph, with gusts into the 30-35 mph range.",
